NOTE whisper small.en

00:00:00.270 --> 00:00:08.110
Being effective in developing your deep neural nets requires that you not only organize your parameters well, but also your hyper parameters

00:00:08.230 --> 00:00:14.870
So what are hyper parameters? Let's take a look. So the parameters of your model are W and B and

00:00:15.670 --> 00:00:18.950
There are other things you need to tell your learning algorithm

00:00:19.630 --> 00:00:21.190
such as

00:00:21.190 --> 00:00:23.190
the learning rate alpha

00:00:24.690 --> 00:00:28.950
Because you need to set alpha and that in turn will

00:00:29.590 --> 00:00:32.150
determine how your parameters evolve or

00:00:32.790 --> 00:00:37.190
Maybe the number of iterations of gradient descent you carry out

00:00:37.670 --> 00:00:44.470
Your learning algorithm has other you know numbers that you need to set such as the number of hidden layers

00:00:46.270 --> 00:00:50.410
So we call that capital L or the number of hidden units

00:00:51.110 --> 00:00:52.590
right

00:00:52.590 --> 00:00:57.030
Such as you know and one and two and so on

00:00:57.750 --> 00:01:00.070
And then you also have the choice of

00:01:00.750 --> 00:01:02.470
activation function

00:01:02.470 --> 00:01:07.390
Do you want to use a relu or tanh or a sigmoid or something especially in the hidden layers?

00:01:07.390 --> 00:01:14.340
And so all of these things are things that you need to tell your learning algorithm

00:01:14.340 --> 00:01:17.100
And so these are parameters that

00:01:17.820 --> 00:01:24.460
Control the ultimate parameters W and B and so we call all of these things below hyper parameters

00:01:25.300 --> 00:01:32.940
Because these things like alpha the learning rate the number of iterations number of hidden layers and so on these are all parameters

00:01:33.700 --> 00:01:38.300
That control W and B. So we call these things hyper parameters

00:01:39.740 --> 00:01:47.440
Because it is the hyper parameters that you know somehow determine the final value of the parameters W and B that you end up with

00:01:48.300 --> 00:01:52.240
In fact deep learning has a lot of different hyper parameters

00:01:53.040 --> 00:01:58.840
Later in the later course, we'll see other hyper parameters as well such as the momentum term

00:01:59.400 --> 00:02:01.400
the mini bash size

00:02:05.670 --> 00:02:11.580
Various forms of regularization parameters and so on and

00:02:12.300 --> 00:02:17.360
If none of these terms at the bottom makes sense yet, don't worry about it. We'll talk about them in the second course

00:02:18.100 --> 00:02:24.260
Because deep learning has so many hyper parameters in contrast to earlier errors of machine learning

00:02:24.980 --> 00:02:32.180
I'm going to try to be very consistent in calling the learning rate alpha a hyper parameter rather than calling a parameter

00:02:32.180 --> 00:02:36.500
I think in earlier errors of machine learning when we didn't have so many hyper parameters

00:02:37.180 --> 00:02:43.940
most of us used to be a bit sloppier and just call alpha a parameter and technically alpha is a parameter but it's a

00:02:44.340 --> 00:02:49.120
parameter that determines the real parameters. I've tried to be consistent in calling

00:02:49.820 --> 00:02:53.620
These things like alpha the number of iterations and so on hyper parameters

00:02:53.700 --> 00:02:56.100
So when you're training a deep net for your own application

00:02:56.300 --> 00:03:02.400
You find that there may be a lot of possible settings for the hyper parameters that you need to just try out

00:03:02.540 --> 00:03:08.260
So apply deep learning today is a very empirical process where often you might have an idea

00:03:08.660 --> 00:03:12.660
For example, you might have an idea for the best value for the learning rate

00:03:12.660 --> 00:03:19.020
You might say well, maybe alpha equals 0.01. I want to try that then you implement it

00:03:19.300 --> 00:03:24.060
Try it out and then see how that works and then based on that outcome you might say, you know what?

00:03:24.060 --> 00:03:27.460
I've changed my mind. I want to increase the learning rate to 0.05

00:03:27.460 --> 00:03:32.100
And so if you're not sure what's the best value for the learning rate to use?

00:03:32.260 --> 00:03:38.100
You might try one value of the learning rate alpha and see the cost function j go down like this

00:03:38.380 --> 00:03:44.020
Then you might try a larger value for the learning rate alpha and see the cost function blow up and diverge

00:03:44.020 --> 00:03:47.340
Then you might try another version and see it go down really fast

00:03:47.340 --> 00:03:51.540
But converge to a higher value and you might try another version and see it

00:03:51.540 --> 00:03:56.040
You know see the cost function j do that then after trying to set the values you might say, okay

00:03:56.040 --> 00:04:03.700
It looks like this the value of alpha gives me a pretty fast learning and allows me to converge to a lower cost function j

00:04:03.700 --> 00:04:05.700
So I'm going to use this value of alpha

00:04:05.900 --> 00:04:09.380
You saw in the previous slide that there are a lot of different hyper parameters

00:04:09.380 --> 00:04:12.180
And it turns out that when you're starting on the new application

00:04:12.500 --> 00:04:18.620
I actually find it very difficult to know in advance exactly what's the best value of the hyper parameters

00:04:18.820 --> 00:04:24.340
So what often happen is you just have to try out many different values and go around the cycle

00:04:24.340 --> 00:04:28.940
You try out some value maybe try five hidden layers with this many number of hidden units

00:04:29.100 --> 00:04:32.500
Implement that see if it works and then iterate so

00:04:33.060 --> 00:04:40.060
The title of the slide is that apply deep learning is a very empirical process and the empirical process is maybe a fancy way of saying

00:04:40.060 --> 00:04:42.780
You just have to try out a lot of things and see what works

00:04:43.580 --> 00:04:50.380
Another effect I've seen is that deep learning today is applied to so many problems ranging from computer vision to speech recognition

00:04:51.020 --> 00:04:57.860
to natural language processing to a lot of structured data applications such as maybe a online advertising or

00:04:58.500 --> 00:05:00.300
web search or

00:05:00.300 --> 00:05:05.900
product recommendations and so on and what I've seen is that first I've seen

00:05:06.140 --> 00:05:13.500
researchers from one discipline any one of these try to go to a different one and sometimes the intuition is about hyper parameters carries over and

00:05:13.700 --> 00:05:19.180
Sometimes it doesn't so I often advise people especially when starting on a new problem to just

00:05:19.740 --> 00:05:25.260
Try out a range of values and see what works and the next course will see a systematic way

00:05:25.260 --> 00:05:29.140
We'll see some systematic ways for trying out a range of values

00:05:29.260 --> 00:05:33.620
Right and second even if you're working on one application for a long time, you know

00:05:33.620 --> 00:05:38.460
Maybe you're working on online advertising as you make progress on the problem

00:05:38.460 --> 00:05:43.520
It's quite possible that the best value for the learning rate the number of fitness units and so on might change

00:05:43.520 --> 00:05:48.780
So even if you've tuned your system to the best value of hyper parameters today

00:05:48.820 --> 00:05:53.100
It's possible you find that the best value might change a year from now

00:05:53.100 --> 00:05:59.380
Maybe because the compute infrastructure be it, you know CPUs or the type of GP or running on or something has changed

00:05:59.380 --> 00:06:02.260
But so maybe one rule of thumb is you know every now and then

00:06:02.700 --> 00:06:06.860
Maybe every few months if you're working on a problem for an extended period of time for many years

00:06:07.100 --> 00:06:11.100
just try a few values for the hyper parameters and double check if there's a

00:06:11.220 --> 00:06:16.820
better value for the hyper parameters and as you do so you slowly gain intuition as well about the

00:06:16.900 --> 00:06:19.420
hyper parameters that work best for your problem and

00:06:19.940 --> 00:06:26.660
I know that this might seem like an unsatisfying part of deep learning that you just have to try a lot of value for these hyper parameters

00:06:27.140 --> 00:06:32.740
But maybe this is one area where deep learning research is still advancing and maybe over time

00:06:32.740 --> 00:06:36.540
We'll be able to give better guidance for the best hyper parameters to use

00:06:36.860 --> 00:06:42.860
but it's also possible that because CPUs and GPUs and networks and data sets are all changing and

00:06:43.220 --> 00:06:47.020
It's possible that the guidance won't converge for some time

00:06:47.020 --> 00:06:51.060
And you just need to keep trying out different values and evaluate them on a hold-on-trucks

00:06:51.140 --> 00:06:54.540
validation set or something and pick the value that works with your problem

00:06:54.540 --> 00:06:58.860
So that was a brief discussion of hyper parameters in the second course

00:06:58.860 --> 00:07:04.040
We'll also give some suggestions for how to systematically explore the space of hyper parameters

00:07:04.700 --> 00:07:08.880
But by now you actually have pretty much all the tools you need to do their program exercise

00:07:09.100 --> 00:07:12.380
Before you do that just a share of you one more set of ideas

00:07:12.380 --> 00:07:16.660
Which is I often am asked what does deep learning have to do with the human brain?
